WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.160
Day 5 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.590
Test cases from user stories: the prompt that actually works.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.597
A versioned prompt template that generates cases by design technique and tells you

00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:14.739
what it didn't cover.

00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:20.725
Asking a model to "generate test cases for this story" always returns the same

00:00:20.725 --> 00:00:25.713
thing: a long, even list with repeated cases and two or three rules nobody defined.

00:00:26.013 --> 00:00:28.395
It isn't a model capability problem.

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:33.516
The request doesn't say which rules to work against, which technique to use or when

00:00:33.516 --> 00:00:33.980
to stop.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.661
This guide replaces that request with a three-step flow that mirrors how a good

00:00:39.661 --> 00:00:44.153
analyst works: ask first, then design with technique and finally declare what was

00:00:44.153 --> 00:00:44.652
left out.

00:00:44.952 --> 00:00:49.677
The result isn't a longer list but a shorter, defensible one, where each case has a

00:00:49.677 --> 00:00:51.840
reason and you know which gaps remain.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:54.837
To follow this guide you need:

00:00:55.387 --> 00:00:58.014
The qa-ai-lab repo from day 4.

00:00:58.564 --> 00:01:01.248
A user story with acceptance criteria.

00:01:01.798 --> 00:01:04.750
Business rules in writing, even five lines.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:07.330
This is the setup.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:11.847
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:13.147 --> 00:01:14.183
Step 1.

00:01:14.483 --> 00:01:16.958
Business rules as the source of truth.

00:01:17.508 --> 00:01:19.543
Write the business rules first.

00:01:19.843 --> 00:01:23.595
They are the source of truth; without them the model invents some.

00:01:24.145 --> 00:01:29.126
Before opening the chat, write the rules governing the feature, even if it's five

00:01:29.126 --> 00:01:32.078
lines: limits, possible states, who can do what.

00:01:32.378 --> 00:01:37.056
If they don't exist in writing, that's your first finding and it's worth more than

00:01:37.056 --> 00:01:37.570
any case.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:42.704
In the prompt, say explicitly that those rules are the only source of truth and that

00:01:42.704 --> 00:01:44.200
it must not invent others.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.375
Without that sentence the model fills gaps with what's common in other systems, not

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:49.727
yours.

00:01:51.027 --> 00:01:52.225
Step 2.

00:01:52.525 --> 00:01:54.072
The template in the repo.

00:01:54.622 --> 00:01:58.375
Save the template in prompts/test-design.md.

00:01:58.925 --> 00:02:02.863
Save the prompt as a file with variables for the rules and the story.

00:02:03.163 --> 00:02:07.779
Having it versioned lets you improve it gradually: every time the result fails in a

00:02:07.779 --> 00:02:11.061
new way, you add an instruction and the whole team gets it.

00:02:11.361 --> 00:02:16.250
It also makes work comparable across people, because everyone starts from the same

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:16.727
request.

00:02:17.027 --> 00:02:21.679
It works the same in Spanish; what matters is that the three stages are separate and

00:02:21.679 --> 00:02:23.949
the first one ends with an order to wait.

00:02:25.249 --> 00:02:26.332
Step 3.

00:02:26.632 --> 00:02:28.550
Step one: questions.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.178
Step 1: ask only for ambiguities and questions.

00:02:33.478 --> 00:02:36.093
You or the PO answer them before moving on.

00:02:36.643 --> 00:02:40.581
This stage delivers the most value and almost everyone skips it.

00:02:40.881 --> 00:02:46.530
Ask for ambiguities, contradictions between criteria and missing data, and nothing

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:46.874
else.

00:02:47.174 --> 00:02:50.590
Take the real questions to the product owner before continuing.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.363
Every ambiguity resolved here is a defect that never gets written.

00:02:55.663 --> 00:03:00.250
If the model finds no questions in a story you know is incomplete, that signals

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.373
missing context, and the cases it generates afterwards will be shallow.

00:03:05.673 --> 00:03:06.686
Step 4.

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:09.334
Step two: cases by technique.

00:03:09.884 --> 00:03:15.605
Step 2: ask for cases by technique (partitions, boundaries, decision table, error

00:03:15.605 --> 00:03:17.653
states) in a table with risk.

00:03:18.203 --> 00:03:23.360
With answers in hand, ask for cases naming the techniques: equivalence partitioning,

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.368
boundary values, decision table and error states.

00:03:26.668 --> 00:03:29.875
Require a table with the technique for each case.

00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:34.568
That lets you see at a glance whether one is missing: a table with no boundaries or

00:03:34.568 --> 00:03:36.157
no error states is incomplete.

00:03:36.457 --> 00:03:41.791
It also reduces duplicates, because two cases from the same partition stand out when

00:03:41.791 --> 00:03:42.299
labeled.

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:44.728
Step 5.

00:03:45.028 --> 00:03:47.410
Step three: what it didn't cover.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.831
Step 3: ask for the not-covered list.

00:03:51.131 --> 00:03:53.769
It's the most useful part for your review.

00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:57.410
Ask for a list of what was left out and why.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:03.065
Concurrency, accessibility, performance, permissions and migration of existing data

00:04:03.065 --> 00:04:04.098
usually show up.

00:04:04.398 --> 00:04:08.209
That isn't a flaw in the result: it's information for deciding.

00:04:08.509 --> 00:04:13.056
Some things you'll cover with another kind of test and others you'll accept as risk,

00:04:13.056 --> 00:04:14.897
but the decision becomes explicit.

00:04:15.197 --> 00:04:19.786
This list is also the best input for the team conversation about what gets tested

00:04:19.786 --> 00:04:20.749
and what doesn't.

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:23.236
Step 6.

00:04:23.536 --> 00:04:25.210
The three-question review.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.848
Review with three questions: any duplicates? any invented rule? any missing

00:04:30.848 --> 00:04:31.527
negatives?

00:04:31.827 --> 00:04:35.034
Whatever survives goes to your test management tool.

00:04:35.584 --> 00:04:37.966
Review the table looking for three things.

00:04:38.266 --> 00:04:42.843
Duplicates: cases exercising the same partition with another value.

00:04:43.143 --> 00:04:47.616
Invented rules: any expected result you can't trace to a written rule.

00:04:47.916 --> 00:04:53.015
Missing negatives: invalid inputs, insufficient permissions, impossible states.

00:04:53.315 --> 00:04:55.222
Discarding a third is normal.

00:04:55.522 --> 00:05:00.587
Priority is yours to set as well, because the model tends to mark everything as high

00:05:00.587 --> 00:05:00.888
risk.

00:05:01.188 --> 00:05:05.069
What survives goes into your test management tool under your name.

00:05:06.369 --> 00:05:07.474
An example.

00:05:08.024 --> 00:05:12.265
The story says: "as a customer I want to transfer money to another account".

00:05:12.565 --> 00:05:17.930
There are four written rules: minimum amount, daily cap, active destination account

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:19.417
and sufficient balance.

00:05:19.717 --> 00:05:24.189
In step one the model asks what happens if the daily cap is reached in the middle of

00:05:24.189 --> 00:05:28.608
a scheduled transfer and whether the cap is per customer or per account; nobody had

00:05:28.608 --> 00:05:29.193
defined it.

00:05:29.493 --> 00:05:32.770
In step two it returns twenty-two labeled cases.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:37.456
In review, five duplicates from the "valid amount" partition and two assuming a

00:05:37.456 --> 00:05:39.121
nonexistent fee are discarded.

00:05:39.421 --> 00:05:40.655
Fifteen remain.

00:05:40.955 --> 00:05:45.642
Step three declares it didn't cover concurrency between two sessions, and the team

00:05:45.642 --> 00:05:47.471
decides to test that separately.

00:05:48.771 --> 00:05:50.433
The most common mistakes.

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:54.167
Asking for cases without passing the business rules.

00:05:54.717 --> 00:05:58.818
Merging the three steps into one request and losing the questions stage.

00:05:59.368 --> 00:06:03.643
Loading cases into the tool without checking duplicates or invented rules.

00:06:04.193 --> 00:06:06.936
Accepting the priority the model assigns.

00:06:08.236 --> 00:06:10.479
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:11.029 --> 00:06:13.215
The template is versioned under prompts/.

00:06:13.765 --> 00:06:17.320
At least one real ambiguity reached the product owner.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:21.507
Each final case has a technique and traces to a written rule.

00:06:22.057 --> 00:06:25.043
There is a discard list with the reason for each.

00:06:26.343 --> 00:06:28.238
And where AI fell short.

00:06:28.788 --> 00:06:31.473
It assigns high risk to almost everything.

00:06:31.773 --> 00:06:35.328
Prioritizing is a business decision, not a writing task.

00:06:36.628 --> 00:06:38.198
That was day 5.

00:06:38.498 --> 00:06:43.472
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:06:43.472 --> 00:06:44.480
asked questions.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:51.778
Continue with day 6: Synthetic test data with LLMs: realistic, valid and PII-free.
